@meta
id: 03-04
title: Open a line below
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: open-o
par: 27
minutes: 4
vscode: click at the end of the line, press Enter, type
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
`Order` needs a `placedAt: Date;` field, directly under `customerId`.

Adding a new line under the one you are on is a single instruction in vim,
not three. It makes the line, moves you onto it, puts you in insert mode,
and copies the indentation of the line you came from — all of that before
you type a character.

Get the indentation from vim, not from your spacebar.

@keys
| `o` | open a new line below, and start typing on it |
| `A` `Enter` | the long way round: end of line, then a new line |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
There is one key for "I want a new line under this one". It is the first
letter of "open".
--
`o` — lowercase — opens below the current line. You do not need `A` first
and you do not need Enter.
--
`jj` to get onto the `customerId` line, then `o`, type the field with no
leading spaces, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    j j o placedAt: Date; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Type the field without indenting it. `o` already gave you two spaces,
because the line above had two. If you type your own spaces on top of
that you get four and the diff looks like you cannot use an editor.

The comparison worth making is with what you would have done in VS Code:
click to the end of `customerId: string;`, press Enter, then type. Three
actions, one of which needs the mouse or `A` first. `o` is one key and it
is position-independent — the cursor can be anywhere on the line above.

`o` is also the key that makes writing new code in vim feel natural rather
than fiddly. Whole functions get written as `o`, type, `Esc`.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  placedAt: Date;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};
